 "notes": [
  "Every transaction was a real settle(id) on Segundo's contract (deployed under its working name, Till), paying a freelancer what they had earned since the last one. 402 of the 461 that succeeded moved money; the rest landed in the same one-second block timestamp as that tab's previous settle, so there was nothing new to pay.",
  "23 transactions reverted, all out of gas on the same 5 tabs: settling them costs 8,032 more gas than the rest (92,488 instead of 84,456; 129,434 instead of 118,591 for a first payout), and we gave every tab one limit taken from the others (plus 6% to 8%) to keep the bill down, because Monad charges the whole gas limit. The relayer in production estimates each call and adds 25%.",
  "Latency for the bursts is from sending the transaction to this machine receiving the header of the block that holds it over WebSocket (newHeads). For the one-at-a-time run it is from sending to the receipt, polled every 150 ms as the relayer does.",
  "The first wave took 1847 ms just to be accepted by the three public RPC endpoints (new connections); later waves took about 425 ms. The Monad Foundation endpoint allows 50 calls a second per client, so senders were spread over three endpoints.",
  "This does not show parallel execution. Every settle changes the contract's AUSD balance, a single storage slot, so under Monad's optimistic parallel execution these transactions conflict and some are re-executed. What it does show is capacity and speed: the busiest block was 8.1% full.",
  "One key is not the limit on Monad: 48 settles sent at once from a single fresh key with consecutive nonces all landed in one block. The bottleneck is Segundo's relayer today, which sends one transaction and waits for its receipt before the next (1.1 a second).",
  "Test MON spent on the whole exercise (setting up 96 tabs, the runs, closing the tabs and sweeping funds back): 11.68 MON, of which 5.57 MON was the settles themselves."
 ]
